First Author: Ayers
Title: Using machine learning to improve survival prediction after heart transplantation
Source number: 4  ￼

Very short summary: This retrospective UNOS registry study evaluated whether modern machine-learning methods improve 1-year survival prediction after orthotopic heart transplantation. Five key findings were: (1) 33,657 adult primary isolated heart transplant recipients were included; (2) 1-year mortality was 11% (n=3738); (3) after manual and automated filtering, 47 variables were used for model training; (4) the final 400-model ensemble achieved the best discrimination with AUROC 0.764, outperforming logistic regression and each individual ML method; and (5) the ensemble also improved net reclassification by 72.9% and had superior decision-curve performance, with calibration assessed visually by deciles of predicted vs observed risk. Figures showed cohort selection, ensemble training design, ROC comparison, and decision-curve analysis; Table 1 reported balanced training and validation cohorts.  ￼  ￼

