Section 3: Intervention & AI Methods
	•	Primary ML Component: Yes
	•	Study Design: Retrospective Cohort
	•	AI Model Architecture: Ensemble
	•	Algorithm Name: Final ensemble ML model combining deep neural network, logistic regression, AdaBoost, and random forest
	•	Input Variables (Data Modality): Tabular (EMR/Clinical data), Donor metrics
	•	Comparator / Standard of Care: Linear Risk Score (e.g., LAS, EuroSCORE)
	•	Validation Method: Internal Split (Train/Test)
	•	Explainability / Interpretability Used: No
	•	Feature Selection Method: Automated (e.g., LASSO, Stepwise)
	•	Hyperparameter Tuning Reported: No
	•	Section 3 Comments/Quotes: The paper describes a “retrospective study” using UNOS data with patients split into training (80%) and validation (20%) sets. The final model was an ensemble of four algorithm classes: deep neural network, logistic regression, AdaBoost, and random forest, built from 400 total models. Inputs were preoperative donor and recipient registry variables. The main explicit comparator in reported results was a “singular logistic regression,” which best fits the review option of a linear risk-score comparator rather than human expert/guideline. Feature selection combined clinician review and random-forest importance, producing 47 final variables. The paper does not describe SHAP/LIME-style explainability or an explicit hyperparameter-tuning process.  ￼  ￼

